  "body": "Automated worker task `task-20260816-093257-adopted-sweep-adopt-veridian-scripts-213-fix-orc`. Risk tier: tier1.\n\nSuperboss review: The actual code changes are well-constructed (parameterized SQL throughout, enum-validated status/verdict columns, idempotent schema/seed helpers, fail-loud prompt-template resolution, fire-and-forget execution logging that never blocks the real step, subprocess.run used with an argv list rather than shell=True for the find_code.sh call, and a real non-mocked end-to-end test suite for both positive and negative paths) and no .github/workflows/** files are touched. However, this PR's branch base (ab898ec, 2026-08-07) is 231 commits and roughly 8 days behind current origin/main (5a862e1, 2026-08-14), and a real merge-tree simulation (`git merge-tree --write-tree HEAD main`) confirms genuine, unresolved CONFLICT (content) hunks in both PROGRESS.md and, more importantly, superboss-register.py -- the actual code file this task modifies. Because tier1 approval here means 'merge to main autonomously', and the diff as submitted cannot be cleanly merged onto the current main tip, approving it as-is would either fail outright at the `gh pr merge` step or require ad hoc manual conflict resolution never reviewed by this pass. Rejecting rather than approving: the worker/task branch must be rebased (or merged) onto current origin/main, conflicts in superboss-register.py resolved by hand (verifying no silent collision with the ~231 commits of unrelated feature work main has gained since this branch diverged, including its own later, unrelated CLI subcommand and table additions), and the full test suite re-run against the rebased tip before this is re-submitted for review.",
